WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
When a platform team builds and react native,

2
00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.320
they are never shipping a single monolithic application.

3
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:09.520
Platform teams operate a dual track lifecycle,

4
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.160
a dynamically-updateable JavaScript bundle,

5
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.480
runs in parallel with a static compiled native OS binary.

6
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.680
But when the lighter JavaScript stream shifts its requirements,

7
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.040
it shatters the heavier blocks of the compiled application.

8
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.040
Allowing these two tracks to decouple

9
00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.600
and update independently is highly efficient,

10
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.840
but it introduces a technical risk.

11
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.480
If the JavaScript track attempts to call a native component

12
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.800
that does not exist in the installed binary,

13
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.160
the mismatch causes a catastrophic runtime crash.

14
00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.120
To package and distribute codes safely in this environment,

15
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.760
you must build strict boundaries and version gates

16
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.440
that force these two separate tracks

17
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.240
to verify their compatibility before executing.

18
00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.000
We will map out a deployment pipeline

19
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
that accomplishes exactly this.

20
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.160
We will use metadata sidecars to secure over-the-air updates

21
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.400
and enforce strict semantic versioning

22
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.720
for publishing to private registries.

23
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.800
Acknowledging this dual track reality

24
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.320
is the prerequisite for scaling a mobile platform team.

25
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.440
Without these safeguards, the version drift between the two tracks

26
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.600
becomes an architectural certainty,

27
00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.680
leading to production failures.

28
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.160
Over-the-air or OTA updates bypass the app store entirely.

29
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.400
They work by replacing the JavaScript bundle

30
00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.160
directly on the user's device while the application is running.

31
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.760
This bypass route operates under a strict legal constraint.

32
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:30.640
App Store review guidelines mandate that OTA updates

33
00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.680
can only deliver behavior-preserving bug fixes.

34
00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.280
You cannot push new, un-reviewed features through this channel.

35
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.760
Beyond compliance, there is a mechanical danger.

36
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.200
It attempts to invoke a native turbo-module method

37
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.480
and the user hasn't downloaded the latest app store binary

38
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.920
containing that method.

39
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.840
The application will fail.

40
00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.720
The solution is generated at compile time.

41
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.080
By embedding specific bundle metadata into the release,

42
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.120
we can explicitly track which production code versions

43
00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.880
are meant to run together.

44
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.920
This diagram outlines the output of the metrobundler

45
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:06.240
operating within the CI pipeline.

46
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.400
It processes the raw source code,

47
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.480
outputting two distinct files,

48
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.480
the large main.js bundle containing the compiled JavaScript,

49
00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.680
and a smaller metadata.jsonsidecar.

50
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:21.760
Inside this jsonsidecar is a specific critical piece of data,

51
00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.760
the min-native version key.

52
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.200
When this update reaches the end user's device at startup,

53
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:31.520
the incoming json file is intercepted by a custom OTA loader module

54
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.240
before any application logic executes.

55
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:36.960
This loader acts as a mechanical safety gate.

56
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.840
It reads the min-native version from the sidecar

57
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.280
and compares that requirement

58
00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.120
against the version of the native binary

59
00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.200
currently installed on the device.

60
00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.600
If the user's native binary is too old,

61
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.200
the gate stays closed.

62
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.040
The OTA update client completely rejects the incoming js bundle

63
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.960
and the system safely falls back to its previous working state.

64
00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.880
Conversely, if the installed binary is an exact match or newer,

65
00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.280
the gate opens, allowing the new js bundle

66
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.680
to safely overwrite the runtime code.

67
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.680
By packaging this bundle metadata,

68
00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.320
you shift the responsibility for application stability

69
00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.880
away from hoping users have updated their apps

70
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.400
to strict, mathematically verifiable client-side gating.

71
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.160
Securing the end user application

72
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.400
is only half the distribution equation.

73
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.640
Platform teams also need to safely package

74
00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:25.200
internal react native libraries and SDKs

75
00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.400
to share with multiple development teams across the organization.

76
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.640
The standard infrastructure for this distribution

77
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.880
relies on private NPM registries,

78
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.720
such as jfrog artifactory, nexus, or github packages.

79
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.280
The first requirement for automated CI publication

80
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.160
to these registries is establishing secure authentication

81
00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:43.920
using a scoped access token.

82
00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.760
This token must be retrieved dynamically

83
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.600
from a secret manager at runtime.

84
00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.000
Hard coding credentials directly into the pipeline script

85
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:53.040
exposes the entire registry to unauthorized access.

86
00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:55.040
This diagram maps the required anatomy

87
00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.040
of a react native library package.

88
00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:59.280
Because the SDK straddles two environments,

89
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.240
it must encapsulate standard JavaScript folders alongside

90
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.520
explicit native iOS and Android configuration files.

91
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.560
Pay specific attention to the cocopods.podspec file

92
00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.400
included in the root of the library.

93
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.560
This file maps the required native dependencies

94
00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.160
to the overarching package.

95
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.320
When a consumer app executes the pod install command,

96
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.400
the application automatically detects

97
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.200
that.podspec inside the NPM package

98
00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:25.040
and links the native dependencies

99
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:27.760
straight into its own iOS directory.

100
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.360
Properly structuring the package

101
00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:31.440
with these native configuration files

102
00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.200
allows downstream consumer apps

103
00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.000
to treat complex dual track SDKs

104
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.560
as simple plug-and-play dependencies.

105
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.480
Maintaining these dependencies at scale

106
00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.400
requires strict governance.

107
00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:44.480
Semantic versioning, or semper,

108
00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.720
serves as a rigid operational contract

109
00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:49.920
for any library consumed by multiple applications.

110
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.040
In a dual track architecture, your version bump strategy

111
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.320
is dictated entirely by which side of the JavaScript native

112
00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:58.480
boundary the code change occurred on.

113
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.520
This matrix outlines the strict versioning rules.

114
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.120
Purely JS side bug fixes, such as adding a null check,

115
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.520
require only a patch bump, for example,

116
00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.120
moving from 1.2.0 to 1.2.1.

117
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:15.520
Adding a brand new native API, like a turbo module method

118
00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.320
for fetch async, forces a minor bump,

119
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:23.680
moving from 1.2.0 to 1.3.0, altering a native signature

120
00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.080
or removing a native API completely,

121
00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.000
such as renaming a data method, breaks backward compatibility,

122
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:32.000
and dictates a major bump.

123
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.480
The most critical trigger for a major bump

124
00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.520
occurs when a library update introduces a requirement

125
00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.920
for a new native binary or a new underlying pod dependency.

126
00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.080
Failing to log this as a major bump

127
00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:48.320
will directly cause an OTA rejection on the end user's device.

128
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:52.000
To validate these cross boundary changes before they hit production,

129
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.560
platform teams should publish pre-release versions

130
00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.440
to the registry for rigorous automated testing.

131
00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.680
Enforcing this strict versioning matrix

132
00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.760
at the registry level is the only way

133
00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:03.840
to prevent downstream application teams

134
00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:06.560
from inadvertently downloading breaking native changes

135
00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.720
through an automated NPM update.

136
00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.160
Developers publish Simver code to our Tifactory left.

137
00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.800
The CI pipeline runs Metro, Center,

138
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:18.800
and mobile devices evaluate the OTA sidecar, right.

139
00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.280
Tracing backward, safe updates execute

140
00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.480
because CI injects the min-native version metadata.

141
00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:26.640
Possible only when upstream libraries

142
00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.160
strictly adhere to the JavaScript native Simver contract.

143
00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:32.480
Effective react native packaging coordinates

144
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.880
the lifecycle of dynamic runtime scripts

145
00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:37.600
with the constraints of static OS binaries.

146
00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.920
Engineers should audit their own deployment pipelines

147
00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:41.840
using industry standard tools.

148
00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:43.680
Utilize react native builder Bob

149
00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.680
for standardized library scaffolding

150
00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.600
and run source map explorer to execute detailed bundle analysis.

151
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.920
Mastering the packaging and distribution pipeline

152
00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.600
provides the infrastructure necessary

153
00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:56.800
to maintain a resilient enterprise scale platform.

